  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Wednesday...\n\nIncreasing pressure and cold air advection across south central MT \nhas allowed for showers to let up a little early this past \nevening. Lingering moisture is expected to keep light to moderate \nscattered rain showers around into the overnight hours. A weak low\nlevel jet has a small chance to bring a few thunderstorms to \nsoutheast MT overnight, but not expecting anything strong. For \ntoday, instability will lend way to very isolated showers and \nthunderstorms, although forcing is very weak, so not expecting \nanything strong outside of some gusty winds. Energy riding the \nridge Friday brings small precipitation chances across southern MT\n(mainly in the higher terrain). Into Saturday, ensembles show \nweak energy moving in under southwest flow. With this pattern and \nprecipitable water values of an inch plus, some strong \nthunderstorms may be in store Saturday (10-20% chance). \n\nBesides these precip chances, the heat will be the main impact \nFriday through Sunday with temperatures in the 90s to 110 degrees \nF. As a result, an Extreme Heat Watch has been issued for Friday\nthrough Sunday. If you have outdoor plans over the weekend, make \nsure to take the necessary precautions to stay safe. Warm and dry \nconditions along with breezy conditions (wind gusts 20-30 mph) \nnorth and west of Billings will lead to elevated fire weather \nconditions Friday. Those who haven't received recent precipitation\nshould be careful to not cause a spark. With ridging continuing, \nabove average temperatures in the 90s to near 100 continue into \nthe first half of next week. TS",
